      <div class="hero-rock rock-f"></div>
    </div>

    <nav class="hero-nav">
      <button class="hamburger md:hidden" id="hamburger" aria-label="Menu">
        <span class="hamburger-line"></span>
        <span class="hamburger-line"></span>
        <span class="hamburger-line"></span>
      </button>
      <div class="nav-links" id="nav-links">
        <a href="#who" class="nav-link">Who's This For</a>
        <a href="#services" class="nav-link">Services</a>
        <a href="#portfolio" class="nav-link">Projects</a>
        <a href="#contact" class="nav-link">Work With Me</a>
      </div>
    </nav>
    <div class="hero-content">

      <p class="hero-subtitle">A little messenger has informed me...</p>

      <h1 class="hero-title">
        You're seeking students willing to trust you &amp; buy your wisdom
      </h1>

      <p class="hero-subheadline">Not a copywriter to do what AI already does with speed &amp; efficiency</p>

    </div>


  </section>

  <!-- ─── WHO IS THIS FOR ──────────────────────────────────────── -->

  <section id="who" class="who-section">
    <!-- brick wall background with clip-path (separate so ornament isn't clipped) -->
    <div class="who-wall-bg" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <!-- top-left corner ornament -->
    <img src="/frame 1.png" alt="" class="who-rune-tl" aria-hidden="true" />

    <div class="who-heading-row">
      <h2 class="who-heading">So...Why should you hire a copywriter nowadays?</h2>
      <div class="who-torch-mobile-wrap">
        <TorchLight width="12px" class="who-torch-mobile" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="who-grid">
      <div class="who-cell-a-row">
        <TorchLight width="24px" class="who-torch" />
        <p class="who-cell-a">I'm not gonna strike you with "here to increase your revenue" or "AI can't replicate human creativity", it all depends on what you're seeking for your coaching business. If you want a few emails written or a landing page done for you, AI is more than enough for the job.</p>
      </div>
      <!-- empty top-right cell -->
      <span></span>
      <p class="who-cell-b">But if you're looking for a strategic partner who understands you and your coaching program, knows how to speak to your ideal target customers, connect the dots between your offer and their pain points and knows exactly how to leverage AI to build a full stack conversion system and reduce friction rather than being lazy and letting AI do the thinking.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── SERVICES ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="services" class="services-section">
    <div class="services-container">
      <h2 class="section-heading">That's exactly what I'm here to do</h2>
      <p class="services-subheadline">the accursed AI does the building while I MYSELF handle the writing</p>
      <div id="tab-services" class="services-grid">
        <div class="service-card">
          <div class="service-icon-wrap"><span class="service-icon-ring"></span><Icon name="lucide:crosshair" class="service-icon" /></div>
          <h3 class="service-card-title">Identifying the ICA</h3>
          <p class="service-card-text">Before I write a single line, I build your ideal customer avatar — their goals, their desires, where they spend their time, and the exact pains and objections standing between them and your program. Everything after this is decided here. Which funnel fits. Which hooks to test. Which objections the copy has to kill before the call ever happens. Skip it, and every choice that follows is a guess.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card">
          <div class="service-icon-wrap"><span class="service-icon-ring"></span><Icon name="lucide:megaphone" class="service-icon" /></div>
          <h3 class="service-card-title">Traffic Acquisition</h3>
          <p class="service-card-text">Organic and paid are not rivals. Put together properly, both put booked calls on your calendar — so I build for whichever one your business is ready to run. For paid, I script short form creatives with multiple hook variations, built to be split tested against each other. You don't get my best guess. You get a test that tells you which angle your market actually responds to. For organic, I script content on a weekly schedule engineered to do three jobs at once: grow engagement, grow the following, and pull in leads. Most content does one of the three and calls it a win.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card">
          <div class="service-icon-wrap"><span class="service-icon-ring"></span><Icon name="lucide:layout" class="service-icon" /></div>
          <h3 class="service-card-title">Architecting Conversion Systems</h3>
          <p class="service-card-text">There is no best funnel. There is only the funnel that fits what you already have. Which one we build comes down to two questions: is your traffic mostly paid or organic, and do you already have an audience? Some funnels convert best on cold traffic and fall apart on warm. Others are the reverse. Getting this wrong is why most funnel rebuilds fail — the shape was never the problem. VSL funnel for cold traffic. Webinar for an established audience. DM funnel for organic reach. Then I build it. Written and wired by the same person, so nothing gets lost between the copy and whoever was supposed to make it exist.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card">
          <div class="service-icon-wrap"><span class="service-icon-ring"></span><Icon name="lucide:mail" class="service-icon" /></div>
          <h3 class="service-card-title">Money in the Follow Up</h3>
          <p class="service-card-text">They followed you on Instagram. They opted in and watched your VSL. And still they didn't book. It is almost never because they can't afford you. It's that it hasn't clicked yet — they are not convinced you're the best person to solve their problem. That's what follow up is for. Email sequences that keep making the case after they've left the page, so the ones who weren't ready today don't quietly become your competitor's client next month. You already paid to get these people. This is how you stop losing them.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

